Agent Response: Thanks — I can walk through this and show how things generally work for 2023. A few quick clarifying questions before I finalize numbers and recommendations:
- Does your spouse have any income (W-2, self-employment, interest, etc.) for 2023? That affects the student‑loan interest phaseout, QBI thresholds, and whether itemizing vs standard is different.
- Are the $4,800 student‑loan payments interest you actually paid in 2023 (not 2018–2019)? Do you have a Form 1098‑E from FedLoan for 2023?
- The $650 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those expenses related to your marketing work (self‑employed), or were they employee expenses for a W‑2 job?
- Do you have any state income tax or property tax paid in 2023, mortgage interest, or other deductible expenses not listed?

Below I assume spouse has no other income and that the $4,800 is 2023 interest payments and the $650 is business (self‑employed) expenses. I’ll show the reasoning so you can adjust if facts differ.

1) Which deductions are available to you
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest paid in 2023 may be deducted as an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1). If your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) for MFJ is between $145,000 and $175,000 the deduction phases out; above $175k you get none. Based on the income numbers you gave (combined = $95,650) you would be under the phaseout if your spouse has little/no income. Even though you paid $4,800, the maximum deduction is $2,500 (assuming eligibility).
- Charitable contribution: Your $1,400 gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction (Schedule A), with normal substantiation rules (you have the receipt — good).
- Unreimbursed expenses: If these $650 are business expenses related to the self‑employment marketing income, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Home office: Because you have self‑employment income ($11,200 marketing), you can claim a business use of home deduction for the portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for that business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Regular (actual) method: allocate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business: 140 sq ft / 780 sq ft = 17.9487% (≈ 17.95%). You pay $2,100/month rent = $25,200/year — business portion of rent ≈ 0.1795 × $25,200 ≈ $4,528. You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to the deduction (but not your spouse’s W‑2 withholding). Because your rent portion alone (~$4,528) > simplified ($700), the regular method will likely be better if you have nothing else to allocate. (If you owned the home, depreciation could further increase the actual‑method deduction, but you said you pay rent.)
  - The home office deduction reduces net Schedule C profit (and thus income subject to income tax and self‑employment tax).
- Self‑employment tax: Your net self‑employment income (Schedule C net) is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: If your marketing income is a qualified trade or business, you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A) equal to 20% of your qualified business income (after allowable deductions). Because your taxable income here is well below the thresholds that would cause wage/property limits to apply, you would likely be eligible for the simple 20% QBI calculation. QBI is calculated after Schedule C deductions (including home office and business expenses) and after the half‑SE tax deduction.

2) Quick numerical illustration (estimates)
Using the numbers you gave and assuming spouse has no income and the $650 is business expense:

- Gross income:
  - W‑2: $82,350
  - Marketing (self‑employment) income: $11,200
  - Interest (Marcus): $1,780
  - Interest (TD Ameritrade): $320
  - Total = $95,650

- Schedule C deductions (if using actual home office method):
  - Home office (rent portion ≈ 17.9487% of $25,200) ≈ $4,528
  - Other business expenses (the $650) = $650
  - Net Schedule C income ≈ 11,200 − 4,528 − 650 = $6,022

- Self‑employment tax (estimate):
  - Taxable SE base = 92.35% × $6,022 ≈ $5,562
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $5,562 ≈ $850
  - Half SE tax deductible ≈ $425 (above the line)

- Student loan interest deduction:
  - Limited to $2,500 (assuming MAGI is under phaseout range). If your MAGI is over the threshold, the allowed amount may be reduced.

- QBI:
  - QBI = net self‑employment income ≈ $6,022
  - QBI deduction ≈ 20% × $6,022 ≈ $1,204 (subject to exact taxable income computation and limits)

- Itemize vs standard:
  - Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700
  - Your itemizable deductions shown = charitable $1,400 (+ maybe state income or property taxes if you have them)
  - Because your itemized total (~$1,400) is far less than $27,700, you should take the standard deduction unless you have significant other itemizable deductions (state taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.).

Conclusion from these estimates:
- Likely take the standard deduction.
- Use Schedule C for the $11,200 marketing income and deduct the home office (actual method appears better than simplified given your rent) and the $650 business expenses.
- Claim student loan interest as an adjustment up to $2,500 (if payments were made in 2023 and your MAGI is under the phaseout).
- Expect some self‑employment tax due on the small net profit; you can deduct half of that on Schedule 1.
- Consider the QBI deduction — it will likely be available and provides additional benefit (~20% of your net business income).

3) Forms you will likely need (2023)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — to report interest income entries and the student loan interest adjustment and half of SE tax as an adjustment
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your marketing (self‑employment) income and business expenses (home office deduction entered on Schedule C)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular (actual) method for the home office; if you use the simplified method you generally just enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C Line 30 (and follow instructions) and do not fill 8829
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (likely you will NOT, based on the numbers provided)
- Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income Deduction) — to compute the QBI 20% deduction (small-business taxpayers generally use Form 8995; Form 8995‑A is for more complex situations)
- 1099‑INT forms (from Marcus and TD Ameritrade) — include interest on Form 1040 (and keep your 1099s)
- Form 1098‑E — student‑loan interest statement from FedLoan (use to substantiate the student loan interest deduction). If you don’t receive 1098‑E, keep records of interest paid.

4) Recordkeeping and practical items
- Keep receipts for charitable giving (your $1,400 receipt is required if you ever itemize) and retain documentation for student‑loan interest (1098‑E), rent/utility bills (for home office allocation), and business expenses.
- If you didn’t receive 1099‑NEC for the $11,200, you still must report the income.
- Because you have SE income and may owe SE tax, check whether you need to make estimated tax payments or increase withholding from your W‑2 pay to avoid underpayment penalties.
- If the $650 were employee unreimbursed expenses, they’re not deductible for tax years 2018–2025; if they’re business expenses, they belong on Schedule C.

5) Recommended next steps
- Confirm spouse income and whether the $4,800 interest was paid in 2023 and is shown on 1098‑E.
