Thanks — I can walk through this and give clear, practical recommendations. I’m not a CPA or tax attorney, so treat this as general guidance and I recommend confirming with tax software or a tax professional before filing.

Summary of your situation (2023, married filing jointly, Chicago, IL)
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Self-employment / contract marketing income (2 clients): $11,200
- Interest: Marcus savings $1,780
- Investment distributions (TD Ameritrade): $320
- Rent: $2,100/month for a 780 sq ft rental (annual rent $25,200)
- Home office: 140 sq ft used exclusively for business
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (FedLoan)
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 (you have receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $650 — please confirm whether these are business expenses you paid for your marketing work (if so, they’re business expenses). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job), those are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

What you can deduct (high‑level)
- Student loan interest: Up to the annual limit (the deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment to income; there is a maximum amount allowed each year). It is subject to income limits. (You paid $4,800, but the allowable deduction is limited by the tax law maximum for the year.)
- Charitable contributions: Your $1,400 cash gift is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize.
- Self‑employment business expenses: Ordinary and necessary expenses for your marketing work (e.g., the $650 you listed if they are business-related) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office deduction: Allowed only if you are self‑employed (Schedule C) and the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business. You can use either the simplified method or the regular method:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot × 140 sq ft = $700 (maximum 300 sq ft).
  - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, etc.) by the business-use percentage = 140/780 ≈ 17.95% of rent/utilities. With your rent ($25,200/year), 17.95% of rent = about $4,523. You may also include proportional utilities if you paid them. The regular method usually gives a larger deduction for renters, but the home-office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business.
- Self‑employment tax: You must pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE), but you may deduct one-half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from your marketing business (complex rules apply). Because your business income is modest, you are likely eligible, but confirm with software or a preparer.

Which is better — itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2023, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is much larger than your reported itemizable deductions.
  - Your itemizable deductions shown: charitable $1,400 (plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc., which you did not list).
  - Standard deduction (MFJ for 2023): $27,700.
- Conclusion: Take the standard deduction. It will almost certainly be larger than your itemized deductions based on what you provided.

How to treat the home office (practical calculations and choices)
- Only available if you are self‑employed (not available for employees with W‑2 income).
- Two methods — simplified (easy) or regular (typically larger for renters with high rent):
  - Simplified: $700 home-office deduction (140 sq ft × $5).
  - Regular: 140/780 = 17.95% × annual rent $25,200 = ≈ $4,523. (You could also include the proportional share of utilities if you pay them.)
- The home-office amount reduces your Schedule C net profit. That lower net profit reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Because your rental amount is large relative to the business income, the regular method is likely more beneficial unless other factors (recordkeeping simplicity) favor the simplified method. Also ensure you meet the exclusive-and-regular use test (the space must be used only for business).

Estimated numeric example (illustrative; round numbers)
I’ll show two simple scenarios — one using the simplified home office ($700) and one using the regular method (rent‑based $4,523). I assume the $650 are legitimate business expenses.

Scenario A — Simplified home office
- Business gross: $11,200
- Business expenses (non‑home): $650
- Home‑office (simplified): $700
- Schedule C net profit = 11,200 − 650 − 700 = 9,850
- Net earnings for SE tax = 9,850 × 92.35% ≈ 9,096
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 9,096 × 15.3% ≈ $1,392
- Deduction for one‑half SE tax (above‑the‑line) ≈ $696
- Student loan interest: you paid $4,800 but the annual deduction is capped (see below); assume you can claim the maximum allowable (the law caps it each year).
- AGI estimate (rough): W‑2 82,350 + interest 2,100 + Schedule C net 9,850 = 94,300, minus above‑the‑line deductions (student loan interest allowed; half SE tax) → AGI roughly in the low $90k range.
- Taxable income after standard deduction (~$27,700) ≈ about $63k (very approximate).

Scenario B — Regular home office (rent‑based)
- Home‑office portion of rent ≈ $4,523
- Business expenses: 650 + 4,523 = 5,173
- Schedule C net profit = 11,200 − 5,173 = 6,027
- Net earnings for SE tax = 6,027 × 92.35% ≈ 5,568
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 5,568 × 15.3% ≈ $852
- Half SE tax deduction ≈ $426
- AGI estimate roughly in the high $80k range
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ about $59–60k (approximate).

Takeaway from the examples
- The regular method (allocating rent) gives a larger home‑office deduction, lowering both income subject to tax and self‑employment tax — so it will likely give you a lower overall tax bill than the simplified method.
- In both examples, the standard deduction ($27,700) is far larger than your itemized deductions, so you should take the standard deduction for 2023.

Forms you will need (for filing 2023 tax year)
- Form 1040 — U.S. Individual Income Tax Return
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — Additional income and adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction and deductible part of self‑employment tax are reported here)
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss From Business (report your $11,200 and expenses, including home office)
- Schedule SE (Form 1040) — Self‑Employment Tax (calculate SE tax)
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize; seems unlikely here)
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC: Report any 1099s you received for contract income (you’ll use them to populate Schedule C)
- Form 1099‑INT (from Marcus) — report interest income
- Form 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (from TD Ameritrade) — report dividends/brokerage proceeds as applicable
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (you should receive this from FedLoan; use it to substantiate the student loan interest deduction)
- If you qualify and claim QBI, Form 8995 or 8995‑A (Qualified Business Income Deduction) may be needed.

Other important notes and documentation
- Keep receipts and records: receipts for charitable gifts, records of home rent and utility bills, records showing exclusive regular use of the home office, business receipts for the $650 and any other business expenses, 1098‑E for student loan interest.
- Confirm whether the $650 are business expenses; if they were unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law.
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe both income tax and SE tax on the net profits of the marketing work; you can deduct half the SE tax on Schedule 1.
- Estimated tax payments: if you expect similar self‑employment income in 2024, consider making quarterly estimated tax payments so you don’t face underpayment penalties.
- QBI deduction: you may qualify for a 20% deduction on qualified business income. This can further reduce taxable income; the calculation is somewhat complex — consider tax software or a preparer.

Recommended next steps
1. Confirm that the $650 are business expenses (not unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses).
2. Gather forms: W‑2, 1099s, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, Form 1098‑E, receipts for charitable donations, proof of rent and utility bills, records showing exclusive business use of the 140 sq ft space.
3. Decide whether to use simplified or regular home‑office method. Based on rent and the math above, the regular method looks more favorable, but check that your business net profit is large enough to absorb the home office deduction.
4. Prepare and file Form 1040 with Schedules C and SE, Schedule 1 for adjustments, and Form 8995 if claiming QBI.
5. Consider using tax software (it will walk you through Schedule C, SE tax, home-office methods, and QBI) or consult a CPA to confirm everything and for state return implications (Illinois return).

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers more precisely for you if you confirm whether the $650 are business expenses and whether you have any additional deductible items (state taxes paid, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc.).
