- Student loan interest: you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI limits). With the income numbers you gave, you’ll likely be eligible for the full $2,500 (you paid $4,800, but the deductible amount is capped).
- Charitable $1,400 only helps if you itemize; since your likely total itemized deductions are small, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025 unless they qualify under a rare exception. If those $650 are business expenses for your self-employed marketing work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown and calculations (example using the numbers you provided)
1) Income
- W-2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self-employment (marketing): $11,200 (assumed Schedule C)
- Interest: Marcus savings $1,780 + TD Ameritrade $320 = $2,100 (since total interest > $1,500 you’ll likely need Schedule B)
Total gross income = 82,350 + 11,200 + 2,100 = $95,650

2) Self-employment and home office
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive out of 780 sq ft total = 140/780 ≈ 0.1795 (≈17.95%)
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200
- Home-office share of rent (actual-expense method) ≈ 25,200 × 0.1795 ≈ $4,527
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700
Which to use?
  - The actual method (use Form 8829) lets you allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. It will likely give a larger deduction here because rent share (~$4,527) > simplified $700.
  - The home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit. It cannot create or increase a Schedule C loss that you couldn’t otherwise have — it’s limited by net earnings from that business.
Example if your unreimbursed $650 are business expenses (rather than employee expenses):
  - Business gross = $11,200
  - Business expenses = $650 (other business expenses) + $4,527 (home-office share) = $5,177
  - Net business profit ≈ $11,200 − $5,177 = $6,023
  - Net profit is subject to income tax and self-employment tax.
  - Self-employment (SE) tax estimate: SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × net profit) — roughly: net 6,023 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $850; half of that (~$425) is an above-the-line deduction.
If instead the $650 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job: they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under the TCJA suspension (with very limited exceptions). In that case do not include them on Schedule A.

