- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $11,200 marketing income and related business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute self-employment tax on Schedule C net profit
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for the home office (if you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because your interest income totals $2,100 (> $1,500)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t seem likely based on provided numbers)
- State (Illinois) return: IL Form IL‑1040 — Illinois has its own rules; interest and business income will generally be reported on your IL return as well. (Illinois has a flat tax rate; check state rules or ask a state tax pro for specifics.)

Additional items and recordkeeping
- Keep receipts for charitable contributions (you said you have the receipt). For cash contributions, a bank record or receipt is needed.
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) from your loan servicer.
- Keep 1099-INT statements from Marcus and TD Ameritrade and any 1099-NEC / 1099‑MISC if you received one from clients for the marketing work (or if you received no 1099, still report the income).
- Keep documentation for the home office (floor plan, photos, records of square footage calculation) and receipts for rent, utilities, insurance in case of audit.
- If you are self-employed, consider quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

