1) Treatment of each item (federal)
- W-2 wages: Report on Form 1040 (W-2 attached).
- Self‑employment income ($11,200): Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You may deduct business expenses on Schedule C (see below).
- Unreimbursed expenses ($650):
  - If these are business expenses related to your self-employed marketing work, report them on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses (reduces Schedule C profit).
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- Interest from Marcus ($1,780): Report as taxable interest on Form 1040 (you should receive a 1099‑INT).
- TD Ameritrade ($320): Could be interest, dividends, or sale proceeds. Each is reported differently:
  - 1099‑INT → taxable interest
  - 1099‑DIV → ordinary/qualified dividends (may get favorable tax rate for qualified)
  - 1099‑B → proceeds from sales (capital gains/losses)
  Confirm which type you received so it’s reported correctly.
- Student loan interest ($4,800): The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (adjustment to income) and is subject to MAGI phaseouts. If your joint MAGI (after adjustments) is below the phaseout range, you can claim up to $2,500; you cannot deduct the full $4,800. You should receive a Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer.
- Charitable gift ($1,400): Deductible only if you itemize (reported on Schedule A).
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive): If you are self-employed, you can take a home office deduction. Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Regular (actual expenses) method: allocate a portion of your rent/utilities/etc. by square footage. Your allocation = 140 / 780 = 17.9487% ≈ 0.17949.
    - Rent deduction = $25,200 × 0.17949 ≈ $4,524
    - You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase deductible amount if you use actual-expense method.
  - Because $4,524 (actual rent portion) > $700, actual-expense method likely yields a larger deduction for you. The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus self-employment tax and taxable income).
- Self-employment tax: Net earnings from self-employment (Schedule C net) are subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute SE tax on Schedule SE; half of the SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Form 1040/Schedule 1.

2) Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Your only reported potential itemized amounts: charitable $1,400 (plus you might have property tax, mortgage interest, state income taxes, medical expenses, etc. — but you did not list any).
- Unless you have substantial mortgage interest, property tax, or other itemizable expenses > $27,700 (unlikely with the numbers shown), you should take the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (reduces AGI) and is independent of whether you itemize.

3) Example rough calculation (estimates) — shows effect of home office + SE tax + student loan interest
Assumptions: the $650 are business expenses; TD Ameritrade $320 = interest; spouse has no other income (you should supply spouse income for accuracy).

- Self-employment gross: $11,200
- Business expenses: $650
- Home office allocation (rent portion): ≈ $4,524
- Schedule C net profit ≈ 11,200 − 650 − 4,524 = 6,026
- SE taxable earnings = 6,026 × 0.9235 ≈ 5,566
