- AGI after half-SE-tax adjustment but before student loan interest ≈ 90,476 − 426 = 90,050
- Student loan interest deduction: maximum allowed is $2,500 (not $4,800), subject to MAGI phaseout. With these numbers (and no spouse income) you’re well below phaseout — likely allowed full $2,500.
- Taxable income: subtract standard deduction $27,700 (if you take it) and the $2,500 adjustment, etc. (this is illustrative — I can run a precise tax calculation once I have spouse income and confirm types of the TD Ameritrade amount and whether the $650 are business expenses).

4) Forms you’ll likely need (federal + Illinois)
Federal:
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest and part of SE adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report self-employment income and business expenses, including home office deduction (or Form 8829 if using actual expenses? — Note: Form 8829 is for business use of home when you have net profit; it helps compute allowable home office; software often handles this.)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (you may receive from your clients) — used to populate Schedule C
- 1099‑INT (Marcus) and 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (TD Ameritrade) — report interest/dividends/capital gain info on 1040
- If eligible for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction, Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) to compute 20% QBI deduction — eligibility depends on taxable income and type of business (I can check once spouse income is known).
Illinois:
- IL-1040 (Illinois individual income tax return). Illinois tax is flat (4.95% for 2023), and many federal adjustments don’t apply; you’ll report federal AGI and make state modifications as required.
- Keep receipts and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) for documentation.

5) Home office specifics and recordkeeping
- The space must be used regularly and exclusively for business. You said 140 sq ft exclusive — that meets the exclusive-use requirement.
- Choose method:
  - If you use actual-expense method, track rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, and any repairs proportionate to business use. Use Form 8829 or software to calculate.
  - Simplified method is easier but likely smaller ($700 vs ~$4,524).
- Keep photos/measurements, copies of lease/rent payments, utility bills, and a log showing business use if needed.

6) Student loan interest specifics
- Max deduction per year is $2,500 (not more).
- There is a MAGI phaseout: for 2023 MFJ phaseout range is $155,000–$185,000 (if your MAGI is above that, deduction is reduced or eliminated). With only the incomes you provided (and no spouse income), you are below the phaseout, but if your spouse has income, it could matter.
- You should have Form 1098‑E from FederalLoan showing interest paid.

7) Estimated/quarterly tax payments
- Because you have self-employment income, you may owe self-employment tax and income tax not covered by withholding. If you expect to owe $1,000+ at filing, you should consider quarterly estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties. With the amounts shown, estimated payments may be small, but I can estimate whether you need them.

8) What I need from you to finalize and produce an estimate or prepare a draft return
- Spouse’s 2023 income (W‑2s, etc.)
- Confirm the nature of the $320 from TD Ameritrade (interest, dividends, or brokerage sales)
