Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Likely take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700). Your itemizable deductions (as listed) are much smaller than that.
- You can (and should) treat the marketing income as self-employment income: report it on Schedule C, and claim legitimate business expenses (including the home-office portion and the $650 if those are business expenses). That will reduce your taxable/self-employment income.
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (adjustment to income) but limited to $2,500 for 2023 — you cannot deduct the full $4,800 on the return.
- Interest from Marcus and TD Ameritrade is taxable and, because total interest > $1,500, you’ll also file Schedule B.
- Forms you’ll typically need: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule B, Schedule C, Schedule SE, and Form 8829 (if you use the regular home‑office method). Possibly Form 8995 (QBI) depending on the net business income.

Details and calculations (assumptions noted)
1) Income you reported
- W‑2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employment / marketing (2 clients): $11,200 (treated as Schedule C gross receipts)
- Interest (Marcus savings): $1,780
- Interest/Dividends (TD Ameritrade): $320
Total gross (before business expenses, adjustments): $95,650

2) Home‑office treatment
- You said the home office is 140 sq ft exclusive out of 780 sq ft total.
- Business use percentage = 140 / 780 ≈ 17.95%.

Two methods:
- Regular method (recommended here because your rent is high):
  - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
  - Rent portion attributable to home office ≈ 25,200 × 17.95% ≈ $4,526.
  - You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. to the business portion if you have them. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the deduction and attach it.
- Simplified method:
  - $5 × 140 sq ft = $700.
Because the rent-only portion of the regular method (~$4,526) is much larger than $700, the regular method will give a much larger deduction.

Note: If you are self‑employed, the home‑office deduction is taken on Schedule C (and via Form 8829 for the regular method). If those 140 sq ft are not used for your self‑employed marketing activity (i.e., you are an employee and used the space for W‑2 work only), you cannot take the home‑office deduction because miscellaneous itemized employee expense deductions are suspended through 2025.

3) Student loan interest
- You paid $4,800 to FedLoan. For 2023 the student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 maximum and phases out at higher MAGI. With the income levels shown (see below), you should be eligible for the full $2,500 as an adjustment to income (assuming you file MFJ and your MAGI is below the phaseout start for MFJ).

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these $650 are business expenses related to the marketing/self‑employment activity, include them on Schedule C (they reduce your net self‑employment income).
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).

5) Self‑employment tax and half‑SE tax deduction
- Net self‑employment income = gross self‑employment receipts minus allowable Schedule C business expenses (including home‑office portion and the $650 if business).
- You must pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net SE earnings (Schedule SE). You may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

6) Interest reporting
- Because total interest > $1,500 (1,780 + 320 = $2,100), you should complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) for Form 1040.

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- If your net business income is positive, you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). For most small self‑employed taxpayers with taxable incomes well below the phase‑in thresholds, the QBI = 20% × qualified business net income (subject to limitations). This is a complex area — include it when preparing the return or consult a tax preparer.

Quick illustrative estimate (approximate — you should confirm with exact numbers)
Assumptions used below:
- You use the regular home‑office method and allocate $4,526 of rent to business.
- The $650 is a business expense.
- No other business expenses or adjustments other than half SE tax and student loan interest.
Calculations:
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 11,200 − (4,526 + 650) = 6,024
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × 6,024) ≈ $851 (rounding). Half deductible ≈ $425.
- Student loan interest deduction (adjustment) limited to $2,500.
- AGI estimate:
   - Total income before adjustments = 82,350 + 6,024 + 2,100 = 90,474
   - Less adjustments: −2,500 (student loan) −425 (half SE tax) = AGI ≈ $87,549
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700 → Taxable income ≈ $59,849
- Rough federal income tax (2023 brackets) ≈ $6,742
- Add self‑employment tax ≈ $851 → total tax ≈ $7,593 (pre‑withholdings or credits).
This is a rough illustration to show the effects of the home office and deductions. Your actual tax will vary depending on exact business expenses, any other credits, withholdings, pretax benefits, and state tax calculations.

Which is better — standard or itemize?
- From the items you listed, itemized deductions look small (charitable $1,400). Standard deduction for MFJ in 2023 is $27,700, so in the scenario above you should take the standard deduction. (Student loan interest is an adjustment to income regardless of itemizing; home-office is taken on Schedule C, not as an itemized deduction.)

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (for reportable additional income and adjustments such as student loan interest and the deductible half of SE tax)
- Schedule B (interest and ordinary dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $11,200 and business expenses (including home office)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method to compute the home office deduction; attach to your return
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for the QBI deduction if applicable
- Keep 1099s (1099‑NEC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV) and W‑2

State (Illinois)
- IL return: Illinois has a flat rate (4.95% in 2023). State rules differ for some deductions (student loan interest adjustment may not be allowed on IL return); consult IL instructions or your preparer.

Records to gather / action items
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting
- 1099‑INT(s) from Marcus and TD Ameritrade (and 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B if applicable)
- Records of the $11,200 self‑employment receipts (invoices, bank deposits)
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses, including the $650 — confirm they’re business‑related
- Proof of home office (floor plan/photos), lease and rent receipts, utility bills (if using regular method)
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from FedLoan showing total interest paid in 2023
- Receipts for charitable contribution to St. Mary’s Food Bank
- Any 1099‑NEC you received (clients may have issued for payments ≥$600)

