  b) Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses by the business-use percentage. Example with rent:
     - Rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 per year.
     - Business portion of rent = 25,200 × 17.95% ≈ $4,525.
     - You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if those exist and are not already personal-only expenses.
     - For self-employed taxpayers, use Form 8829 to compute the home office deduction (and then the deduction passes through to Schedule C).
- Which to choose: based on the rent alone, the actual method (≈$4,525) is substantially larger than the simplified ($700), so the actual method will usually be better — but you must have documentation of rent and other shared home costs.

Important: If you are an employee (W-2) using a home office for your employer, you generally cannot take a home office deduction for tax years 2018–2025. The home office deduction here only applies if the marketing income is self‑employment (Schedule C) and the office is used exclusively and regularly for that business.

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (Schedule 1), up to $2,500 maximum per year (even if you actually paid more).
- It is subject to modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) phase-outs. Whether you can take the full $2,500 depends on your MAGI. Tell me if you want me to check your MAGI-based eligibility (I can if you want an exact calculation).
