I’m not a tax professional, but based on the information you gave I can show how these items are normally treated, estimate impacts, and list the forms and records you’ll need. For definitive advice and to file, please confirm with a CPA or tax preparer.

Quick summary / recommendation
- You’ll almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) — your itemizable deductions (charity $1,400 plus any SALT, mortgage interest, big medical expenses) are unlikely to exceed that unless you have substantial other deductible items you didn’t list.
- Your student loan interest payment ($4,800 reported) is limited to a maximum $2,500 above-the-line deduction (subject to MAGI phaseouts). Based on the numbers you gave, you’re under the phaseout, so you can likely take the full $2,500.
- Your marketing income is self‑employment income. Treat it on Schedule C. That lets you take a home‑office deduction (actual or simplified) and ordinary business expenses; it also makes you subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- The home office deduction will likely help — using the actual method (allocating a portion of your rent) looks substantially better than the simplified method in your case. The home-office deduction reduces Schedule C profit and thus both income tax and self-employment tax.
- Forms you will need: Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule B (interest) and your Illinois state return (IL‑1040). See the full list below.

Detailed walk-through with rough numbers (estimates)
Inputs you gave:
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self-employment (marketing, 2 clients): $11,200
- Interest: Marcus savings $1,780 + TD Ameritrade $320 = $2,100 total interest
- Business expenses: $650 (you called them unreimbursed — if they’re business expenses for your self‑employment, report them on Schedule C)
- Home: exclusive home office 140 sq ft of 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month = $25,200/year
- Student loan interest paid in 2023: $4,800 (but deduction capped at $2,500)
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 (you have receipts)

Step 1 — Calculate Schedule C profit (self‑employed portion)
Option A — actual home office method (allocating rent):
- Home office percentage = 140 / 780 ≈ 17.95%
- Rent portion attributable to office = 0.1795 * $25,200 ≈ $4,526
- Total business expenses = $650 + $4,526 = $5,176
- Net Schedule C profit = $11,200 - $5,176 ≈ $6,024

Option B — simplified method:
- Deduction = $5 per sq ft * 140 sq ft = $700
- Total business expenses = $650 + $700 = $1,350
- Net Schedule C profit = $11,200 - $1,350 = $9,850

Step 2 — Self-employment tax (Schedule SE) and the above-the-line half-SE deduction
- SE tax is computed on 92.35% of net Schedule C profit, at 15.3%. Half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income.
- Using actual method net SE ≈ $6,024:
  - SE base ≈ $6,024 * 0.9235 ≈ $5,566
  - SE tax ≈ $5,566 * 15.3% ≈ $851
  - Half SE deduction ≈ $426
- Using simplified method net SE ≈ $9,850:
  - SE base ≈ $9,098
  - SE tax ≈ $1,392
  - Half SE deduction ≈ $696

Step 3 — Adjusted gross income (AGI) estimate (illustrative)
Start with total income = W‑2 + net Schedule C + interest
- Actual method total income ≈ $82,350 + $6,024 + $2,100 ≈ $90,474
- Subtract adjustments: half SE deduction (~$426) and student loan interest deduction (limited to $2,500) → AGI ≈ $90,474 - $426 - $2,500 ≈ $87,548
- Simplified method AGI would be higher (approx $91,100) because the business deduction is smaller.

Bottom line from these numbers: the actual home office method (allocating rent) produces a substantially lower AGI and lower tax than the simplified method in your situation. (I used the rent-only allocation for home office; if you have utilities, renter’s insurance, or other allocable household costs those can increase the actual-method home-office deduction further.)

Student loan interest
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (even if you paid $4,800).
- Phases out by MAGI for married filing jointly (2023 phaseout roughly $145k–$175k). Based on the numbers above, your MAGI is well below the phaseout, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500.

Charitable contributions
- Your $1,400 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction ($27,700 for MFJ in 2023) is much larger than $1,400, you will generally take the standard deduction unless other itemizable items (property tax, state income tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses) push your total itemized deductions above $27,700.

Interest reporting
- Because total interest is $2,100 (> $1,500), you’ll complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) and report the 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV amounts.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 — Additional Income and Adjustments (student loan interest deduction and the half SE tax adjustment feed into Schedule 1)
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report marketing income, business expenses, and home office)
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (to compute SE tax)
- Schedule B — Interest and Ordinary Dividends (since interest > $1,500)
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you decide to itemize)
- Keep Form 1099‑NEC or other payor statements for the $11,200 (if issued), Forms 1099‑INT for the $1,780 and $320, and Form 1098‑E for student loan interest
- Illinois state return: IL‑1040 (report federal AGI and any IL adjustments)

Records and documentation to gather
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting
- 1099‑NEC / records showing the $11,200 (or invoices/receipts if not issued)
- Bank statements/1099‑INT for Marcus and TD Ameritrade
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) or loan servicer records showing interest for 2023
- Rent receipts, canceled checks, or bank statements showing rent payments; copy of your lease
- Documentation showing home office size (floor plan, photos) and exclusive use for business
- Receipts for the $650 business expenses
- Receipt or acknowledgment for the $1,400 charitable gift
- Any records of state/local tax paid, mortgage interest, property tax, or other potential itemizable items

Special notes & cautions
- Home-office deduction applies only to self‑employed/business use. If you’re strictly an employee for that activity and your employer did not reimburse you, you cannot take the home-office deduction on your federal return.
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income — you don’t need to itemize to claim it.
- The home office deduction reduces Schedule C profit, which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- If your self‑employment income is ongoing, consider paying estimated taxes (Form 1040‑ES) quarterly to avoid underpayment penalties next year.
- This is a high-level summary. Specifics (e.g., whether those $650 are employee unreimbursed expenses or business expenses) change the result. Also special rules can apply if you received payments through third‑party networks (1099‑K) or had other credits/deductions.

