  - Only allowed if you use the space regularly and exclusively for your business and you are self-employed (Schedule C) or meet very limited qualifying conditions (rare) for employees.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700 deduction. Easy and often preferable for small offices.
    - Regular method: allocate a portion of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) by the business-use percentage (140/780 ≈ 17.95% ≈ 18%). Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; rent portion ≈ $4,526. You would also allocate utilities, etc. The regular method is more complex (Form 8829) and the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business — any excess may be limited and carried forward under certain rules.
